<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0a82d6a06cb9> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Analysis of records from a major US hospital revealed that the algorithm used effectively let whites cut in line for special programs for patients with complex, chronic conditions such as diabetes or kidney problems." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0ce15f01903e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The algorithm is used to 'manage more than 70 million lives.'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/21cbc224a3c4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The new study examined a standard calculation called CKD-EPI used to convert a blood test for a person’s level of the waste product creatinine into a measure of kidney function called estimated glomerular filtration rate, or eGFR." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1192" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3da0bd30a138> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The study, published in _Science_ on 24 October, concluded that the algorithm was less likely to refer black people than white people who were equally sick to programmes that aim to improve care for patients with complex medical needs." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/41baf172c145> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Racial bias in a medical algorithm favors white patients over sicker black patients" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/43cacabdfd7a> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[2062] Mullainathan and his collaborators discovered that the algorithm they studied, which was designed to help health systems target patients who would have the greatest future health-care needs, was predicting how likely people were to use a lot of health care and rack up high costs in the future." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5ae0bf457c33> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Care for some of the sickest Americans is decided in part by algorithm. New research shows that software guiding care for tens of millions of people systematically privileges white patients over black patients." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5c64598a186d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[2062] Mullainathan and his collaborators discovered that the algorithm they studied, which was designed to help health systems target patients who would have the greatest future health-care needs, was predicting how likely people were to use a lot of health care and rack up high costs in the future." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/657af19452f3> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "A Health Care Algorithm Offered Less Care to Black Patients" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/8352d8d86e29> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The problem was caught in an algorithm sold by a leading health services company, called Optum, to guide care decision-making for millions of people." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/92bf02c3868f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Black men were likely underdiagnosed with lung problems because of bias in software, study suggests" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a63199ad44ff> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "How an Algorithm Blocked Kidney Transplants to Black Patients" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1192" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/aa771bea734b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[3040] Earlier this year, the American Thoracic Society, which represents lung-care doctors, issued a statement recommending replacement of race-focused adjustments." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b76e50f378d2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1262] People with lower incomes typically run up smaller health costs because they are less likely to have the insurance coverage, free time, transportation, or job security needed to easily attend medical appointments, says Linda Goler Blount, president and CEO of nonprofit the Black Women’s Health Imperative." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/dc7edbf84a18> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The study, published in _Science_ on 24 October, concluded that the algorithm was less likely to refer black people than white people who were equally sick to programmes that aim to improve care for patients with complex medical needs." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e2772b1f20a3> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Millions of black people affected by racial bias in health-care algorithms" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e503c6984c9c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Suchi Saria, a machine learning and health-care expert at Johns Hopkins University, said the study was fascinating because it showed how, once a bias is detected, it can be corrected." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/f106374cee1f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[2062] The problem was caught in an algorithm sold by a leading health services company, called Optum, to guide care decision-making for millions of people." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/f364a2e907f4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When Obermeyer and his team reported their findings to the algorithm’s developers — Optum of Eden Prairie, Minnesota — the company repeated their analysis and found the same results." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/fcc2fc1d87df> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "It’s often done using a spirometer — a device with a mouthpiece connected to a small machine." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/Risk/65f88fee0d69> a airo:Risk ;
    rdfs:label "Risk of systemic racial bias in health care resource allocation" ;
    airo:confidenceScore "0.9"^^xsd:float ;
    airo:extractionMode "abstracted" ;
    airo:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/043d86154b3f> ;
    airo:hasSourceDocument <https://w3id.org/airo/incident/document/1262> ;
    airo:leadsTo <https://w3id.org/airo/incident/Consequence/7602e93a98cc> .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/043d86154b3f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1262] Care for some of the sickest Americans is decided in part by algorithm. New research shows that software guiding care for tens of millions of people systematically privileges white patients over black patients." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1c146eee9ce9> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The software used to predict patients' need for more intensive medical support was an outgrowth of the Affordable Care Act, which created financial incentives for health systems to keep people well instead of waiting to treat them when they got sick." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4fe1851b5b56> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[2500] The study, published in _Science_ on 24 October, concluded that the algorithm was less likely to refer black people than white people who were equally sick to programmes that aim to improve care for patients with complex medical needs." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/71313ebedcb4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The study, published in _Science_ on 24 October, concluded that the algorithm was less likely to refer black people than white people who were equally sick to programmes that aim to improve care for patients with complex medical needs." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ed31edc1901c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1262] \"There were stark differences in outcomes,\" says Ziad Obermeyer, a physician and researcher at UC Berkeley who worked on the project with colleagues from the University of Chicago and Brigham and Women’s and Massachusetts General hospitals in Boston." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0b5938e5df62> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Suchi Saria, a machine learning and health-care expert at Johns Hopkins University, said the study was fascinating because it showed how, once a bias is detected, it can be corrected." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0dee7f03d642> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Earlier this year, the American Thoracic Society, which represents lung-care doctors, issued a statement recommending replacement of race-focused adjustments." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/21b8b5c21ebc> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The new study looked at more than 2,700 Black men and 5,700 white men tested by University of Pennsylvania Health System doctors between 2010 and 2020." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/398553ddc211> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Ziad Obermeyer, who studies machine learning and health-care management at the University of California, Berkeley, and his team stumbled onto the problem while examining the impact of programmes that provide additional resources and closer medical supervision for people with multiple, sometimes overlapping, health problems." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/81f319b17014> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "While physicians also look at symptoms, lab work, X-rays and family histories of breathing problems, the pulmonary function testing can be an important part of diagnoses, “especially when patients are borderline,” said Dr. Albert Rizzo, the chief medical officer at the American Lung Association." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/d41507d8538f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Neal has asked medical societies and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services to investigate the impact on patients of clinical algorithms that use race." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1192" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e72dd4124fb7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Christine Vogeli, director of evaluation and research at the Center for Population Health at Partners HealthCare, a nonprofit health system in Massachusetts, said when her team first tested the algorithm, they mapped the highest scores in their patient population and found them concentrated in some of the most affluent suburbs of Boston." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e92c93823b53> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "People with lower incomes typically run up smaller health costs because they are less likely to have the insurance coverage, free time, transportation, or job security needed to easily attend medical appointments, says Linda Goler Blount, president and CEO of nonprofit the Black Women’s Health Imperative." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/eb9655166ff6> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Ruha Benjamin, an associate professor of African American studies at Princeton University, drew a parallel to the way Henrietta Lacks, a young African American mother with cervical cancer, was treated by the medical system." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/f16d2c83c23d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Mullainathan and his collaborators discovered that the algorithm they studied, which was designed to help health systems target patients who would have the greatest future health-care needs, was predicting how likely people were to use a lot of health care and rack up high costs in the future." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/fb0c6ec12ec2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "This study, published in JAMA Network Open, offers one of the first real-world examples of how the the issue may affect diagnosis and care for lung patients, said Dr. Darshali Vyas, a pulmonary care doctor at Massachusetts General Hospital." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "3040" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/fcc60086b4e0> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Last month, Senator Elizabeth Warren (D-Massachusetts) and others asked the Department of Health and Human Services to investigate race-based medical algorithms." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1192" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0c7408d0def7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The study analyzed health records for 57,000 people with chronic kidney disease from the Mass General Brigham health system that includes Harvard teaching hospitals Massachusetts General and Brigham and Women's." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1192" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/195e6dc0b566> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When Obermeyer and his team reported their findings to the algorithm’s developers — Optum of Eden Prairie, Minnesota — the company repeated their analysis and found the same results." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2500" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e8f352c1d914> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The problem was caught in an algorithm sold by a leading health services company, called Optum, to guide care decision-making for millions of people." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2062" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0aa1b6b9bd0b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "That suggests the algorithm may be from Optum, owned by insurer UnitedHealth, which says its product that attempts to predict patient risks, including costs, is used to \"manage more than 70 million lives.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1262" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ee2529d7f3f5> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "A preliminary version of the newly published findings helped convince leaders at Mass General Brigham to abandon the race-based eGFR formula in June. Several other major US hospitals, including University of Washington and Vanderbilt, have done the same this year." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1192" .

